          var s = 'Your answers describe the deepest form of the marriage question: not \u201Cwho\u201D but \u201Cwhether\u201D \u2014 whether this is something that happens to people like you at all.';
          if (a.belief === 'stopped-wanting') s += ' You said it plainly: you\u2019ve stopped letting yourself want it. That\u2019s not indifference \u2014 it\u2019s protection, and it usually has a specific history: the waiting that didn\u2019t end, the hope that cost too much. The withdrawal is worth respecting and worth examining, because it changes behavior quietly: fewer chances taken, fewer people met.';
          if (a.belief === 'quietly-no') s += ' \u201CMostly no, quietly\u201D is the belief doing its work in the background \u2014 shaping what you attempt without announcing itself. The honest question isn\u2019t whether the belief is true; it\u2019s what evidence it was built from, and whether that evidence is current.';
          if (a.status === 'long-wait') s += ' And the waiting has been long \u2014 which is exactly why the belief formed. The useful move isn\u2019t reassurance (research on reassurance-seeking shows it gets discounted) and isn\u2019t a forecast. It\u2019s the inventory: what has actually prevented connection \u2014 circumstance, pattern, timing, protection \u2014 and what of that is still true today?';
