          var s = 'Your answers describe the healthy version of this question: the big three held as three mirrors, each checked against your actual life instead of worn as a verdict. Used this way, the trio does what reflective frameworks actually do \u2014 it makes \u201Chow do I come across versus how do I feel versus what am I like at core\u201D specific enough to answer with evidence.';
          if (a.use === 'self-view') s += ' You said a chart description named something you felt but hadn\u2019t framed. That\u2019s the real thing \u2014 and it\u2019s also where the discipline matters: recognition is not verification. The Barnum finding stands behind every appealing placement story, and \u201Call of them feel accurate\u201D is the tell: a description that fits everyone is describing the reader, not the chart. The test that keeps it honest: does the trio generate anything you could have gotten wrong \u2014 a prediction about your instinct under pressure, your presentation with strangers \u2014 that you can check against your life?';
          if (a.trigger === 'deeper') s += ' And you came in because you heard the big three is just the beginning \u2014 which is the concept\u2019s own honest self-description: an introduction, not a conversation. Worth deciding deliberately how deep to go, because the honest answer is \u201Cas deep as the question needs\u201D: a person question may need the full chart; a social one may not need the trio at all.';
